.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Treating a column as N equilibration stages (&quot;theoretical plates&quot;) predicts a Gaussian peak whose width grows as t_R/\sqrt N. theoretical_plates inverts that: N=16(t_R/w_{base})^2=5.545(t_R/w_{1/2})^2, and plate_height gives the plate height H=L/N. Here we measure the widths of simulated peaks and recover the plate count they were built from.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/analytical/chromatography/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_02_martin_synge_theoretical_plates_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/analytical/chromatography/plot_02_martin_synge_theoretical_plates`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">Martin and Synge's theoretical plates from a peak's width</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/analytical/chromatography/plot_02_martin_synge_theoretical_plates`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="purnell_resolution writes resolution as R_s=\frac{\sqrt N}{4}\,\frac{\alpha-1}{\alpha}\,\frac{k_2}{1+k_2}. We check it against the direct peak-width formula resolution on simulated chromatograms, then compare the three levers: doubling N only multiplies R_s by \sqrt2, whereas a small change in selectivity \alpha can do much more.">

